ame><name><surname>Amato</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Resect and discard strategy in clinical practice: a prospective cohort study</article-title><source>Endoscopy</source><year>2012</year><volume>44</volume><fpage>899</fpage><lpage>904</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">22859259</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B34"><label>34</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Gross</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Trautwein</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Behrens</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Computer-based classification of small colorectal polyps by using narrow-band imaging with optical magnification</article-title><source>Gastrointest Endosc</source><year>2011</year><volume>74</volume><fpage>1354</fpage><lpage>1359</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">22000791</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B35"><label>35</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Takemura</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Yoshida</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Tanaka</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Computer-aided system for predicting the histology of colorectal tumors by using narrow-band imaging magnifying colonoscopy (with video)</article-title><source>Gastrointest Endosc</source><year>2012</year><volume>75</volume><fpage>179</fpage><lpage>185</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">22196816</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B36"><label>36</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Rex</surname><given-names>DK</given-names></name><name><surname>Fennerty</surname><given-names>MB</given-names></name><name><surname>Sharma</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Kaltenbach</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Soetikno</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Bringing new endoscopic imaging technology into everyday practice: what is the role of professional GI societies? Polyp imaging as a template for moving endoscopic innovation forward to answer key clinical questions</article-title><source>Gastrointest Endosc</source><year>2010</year><volume>71</volume><fpage>142</fpage><lpage>146</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">19922926</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B37"><label>37</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hogan</surname><given-names>RB</given-names><suffix>3rd</suffix></name><name><surname>Brill</surname><given-names>JV</given-names></name><name><surname>Littenberg</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Demarco</surname><given-names>DC</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Predict, resect, and discard ... really?</article-title><source>Gastrointest Endosc</source><year>2012</year><volume>75</volume><fpage>503</fpage><lpage>505</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">22341098</pub-id></element-citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-group><fig id="F1" position="float"><label>Fig. 1</label><caption><p>A) A 3-mm polyp with strong vascular pattern intensity. Histopathology showed it was an adenoma (courtesy Dr. James East, MD). (B) A 3-mm hyperplastic polyp showing weak pattern intensity (courtesy Dr. James East, MD).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ce-46-120-g001"/></fig><fig id="F2" position="float"><label>Fig. 2</label><caption><p>(A) A 2-mm hyperplastic polyp showing fine capillary network but absent mucosal pattern (bland pattern). (B) A 4-mm hyperplastic polyp showing the circular pattern with dots. (C) A 4-mm adenoma showing the round or oval pattern. (D) A 6-mm adenoma showing the tubulogyrus pattern.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ce-46-120-g002"/></fig><fig id="F3" position="float"><label>Fig. 3</label><caption><p>Lesion with clearly visible meshed capillary vessels, histologically diagnosed as an adenoma (courtesy Dr. Yasushi Sano, MD).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ce-46-120-g003"/></fig><fig id="F4" position="float"><label>Fig. 4</label><caption><p>(A) Capillary pattern I; hyperplastic polyp (courtesy Dr. KI Fu, MD). (B) Capillary pattern II, adenoma with low-grade dysplasia (courtesy Dr. KI Fu, MD). (C) Capillary pattern III, adenoma with high-grade dysplasia (courtesy Dr. KI Fu, MD).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ce-46-120-g004"/></fig><fig id="F5" position="float"><label>Fig. 5</label><caption><p>(A) Hyperplastic polyp. Only very few vessels are visualized on the surface and do not show increased branching (courtesy Dr. J Tischendorf, MD). (B) Polyp showing increased density of irregular, curved and dilated blood vessels. Histologic examination showed adenoma (courtesy Dr. J Tischendorf, MD).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ce-46-120-g005"/></fig><fig id="F6" position="float"><label>Fig. 6</label><caption><p>Features of the narrow band imaging International Colorectal Endoscopic (NICE) criteria: (A) color, (B) vessels, and (C) surface pattern (courtesy Dr. D.K. Rex, MD).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ce-46-120-g006"/></fig></floats-group></article>